/-!
# The actual central factor of the radius-four endpoint layers

On the regular extended path, index 4 is alpha, while indices 0 and 8
are the critical pair (alpha-4,alpha+4). Three literal local turns put
both endpoint Z-layers in the nested span hat W_alpha. Its first hat
layer is contained in V_alpha, which centralizes the entire radius-four
ball since 2+4<8. Thus the central factor is the first hat layer; no
central supplement of the whole radius-four ball is assumed.

For the Frattini containment, the sixth orbit layer is generated by
involutions. Its Frattini subgroup is therefore contained in its derived
subgroup. Monotonicity for finite two-groups and the derived-layer bound
put Phi(core_alpha) inside V_alpha. The moved radius-four subgroup lies
in core_alpha by distance and centralizes Phi(core_alpha) by the same
radius inequality. All finiteness is local in the infinite FreeAmalgam.

This is a conditional reduction, not yet the requested source producer.
The remaining literal hypotheses in `radiusFourFactors_of_extendedPath`
are the following actual source calculations:
* `hmoving`: hat W_alpha <= [hat W_alpha,Ltilde_alpha] join hat V_alpha,
  the generation consequence of the two dual natural quotients in (4.5.4)(iii);
* `hcore`: core_alpha <= hat V_alpha^(6), exactly (4.5.4)(vi);
* `hderived`: the derived subgroup of hat V_alpha^(6) lies in hat V_alpha,
  the upper bound in (4.5.5).

Their existing producer is qt-c01-ms93-core-factors-higher-layers, whose
preceding (4.5.1)--(4.5.3) inputs belong to
qt-c01-ms93-core-factors-early-layers. This task retains their discharge
and final assembly. The allowed preceding inputs `hb` and `p` belong to
qt-c01-ms93-distance-eight-path-centrality: (4.4) and the regular extended
path from (4.4.4)--(4.4.5). Its parent retains that earlier discharge.
No core-center or fixed-factor proof is duplicated here.

`extendedPath_noncommutation` applies the resulting factors to both
published noncommutation consumers. The existing core-isomorphism
consumer then handles transport to the original finite MS core.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, *Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank
two* (1993), (4.4.5), (4.5.4)--(4.5.5), printed pp.894--899, and the
selected path at the beginning of (4.5), pp.895--896. All six scans of
pp.895--900 were checked; (4.5)(iii) centralizes Ltilde, not Z_alpha.
-/

